virglrenderer: encoder frames finish beside QEMU's main loop

What: the VideoToolbox encoder (virgl-videotoolbox-encode.patch) no longer waits
for the Mac's media engine on vrend's thread, which is QEMU's main loop (it also
runs the VM's sound timers and display). Each frame is a job (at most 4 in
flight per encoder): BEGIN_FRAME copies the guest's picture on the GPU and
leaves a GL fence instead of glFinish; ENCODE_BITSTREAM sets the job's options;
the job goes to VTCompressionSessionEncodeFrame once that fence signalled, and
END_FRAME only closes it and numbers it. The output callback finds its job by
id under enc_lock and wakes the main loop (QEMU's poll fd);
virgl_video_encode_poll(), run from vrend_renderer_poll(), sends jobs whose copy
is done and gives finished ones back in order (encode_completed: coded data and
feedback, now written through GL_COPY_WRITE_BUFFER, in context 0). vrend_video.c
keeps each frame's coded-data and feedback buffers in a queue for that. Fences:
each vrend fence records the last job closed before it, and does not signal
before every job up to it went back: the sync thread waits for that after the
GL fence (it wakes the main loop first; 2 s at most, then a warning), polled
fences stay unretired. The guest reads its feedback only after such a fence, so
it never sees a frame half done. Constant QP: a normal session with
MinAllowedFrameQP and MaxAllowedFrameQP at the guest's I and P QP (equal for
FFmpeg and gpu-screen-recorder unless told otherwise); VideoToolbox takes them
only before the first frame, so another QP is another session. Where the
encoder refuses the range, or with OMACVM_VIDEO_ENCODE_BASE_QP=1, the
low-latency session with BaseFrameQP as before (its EncodeFrame waits for the
frame). A frame the media engine has had for 1 s without output, and any frame left
without output after CompleteFrames, fails instead of holding fences.
Unchanged: the frame that never ended fails at the next BEGIN_FRAME,
a second encode in a frame is refused, every attempted frame gets exactly one
result, refused frames fail at once.
Why: screen recording in the VM (Omarchy's gpu-screen-recorder, h264_vaapi,
constant QP, 60 fps of a 2940x1840 display) kept QEMU's main loop busy 93 % of
the time on a MacBook Air M2: 78 % in VTCompressionSessionEncodeFrame (the
low-latency session waits for each frame, 12-13 ms), 6.6 % in glFinish. The
guest's display fell to 12 frames a second and the Mac got about a third of the
sound (6.8 s per 20 s, crackling). With this: the main loop is idle 86 % of the
time while recording, the sound is whole (20.7 s per 20.7 s), the display runs
at 29 frames a second (each frame's fences wait for the media engine) and
gpu-screen-recorder uses 8 % of a vCPU instead of 87 %.
Upstream: OmacVM addition to its own backend; the fence hook would need an
upstream API for late video results. Not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-encode.c waits for a fence after each submit as the
guest does and checks that no fence signals before the frames ahead of it have
their feedback; three frames sent at once, each with its own buffers, all come
back in order and decode; runs with polled fences, with QEMU's sync-thread fences
(OMACVM_TEST_THREAD_SYNC=1) and with the low-latency session
(OMACVM_VIDEO_ENCODE_BASE_QP=1). Tests/virgl/run-regressions.py: the coded-data
write no longer touches vertex state (its review entry is gone);
index-range-writes.py: the write still bumps the buffer's content generation.
